 {"plain": "Around 1 in 200 US adults and about 1 in 70 US teenagers identify as transgender. Not all have dysphoria, and far fewer have the formal diagnosis.", "figure": "About 0.5% of US adults and 1.4% of youth aged 13–17 identify as transgender (Williams Institute, 2022)", "source_ref": "src-williams-trans"},
 {"plain_summary": "Care is individual and should be led by the person. Many people are helped most by social support: using their name and pronouns, changing how they dress or present, and being accepted at home and school. Mental health support helps with distress, anxiety, or depression, and is not aimed at changing identity. Some people choose medical options, such as puberty-pausing or hormone treatment or, in adulthood, surgery, after assessment by specialist teams following international standards such as WPATH's, with all medication prescribed and monitored by a doctor. Access and rules differ a lot between countries, and for under-18s they're changing in several places.",
  "evidence_based_options": ["Social support and affirmation at home, school, and work", "Mental health support for distress, depression, or anxiety, not aimed at changing identity", "Specialist gender services for assessment and, where chosen, medical care under international standards", "Family support programmes and peer support"],
  "self_help_that_supports_care": ["Finding peer community, online or local, run by trusted organisations", "Small changes that ease dysphoria day to day: clothing, hair, name", "Knowing your rights at school and work", "Looking after mental health; dysphoria is tougher when you're isolated"],
  "guideline_refs": ["src-wpath-soc8"]},
